  [38;5;153mrecord.js[39m and [38;5;153mredact.js[39m are pure functions, so they test directly. [38;5;153msink-remote.js[39m needs one design accommodation to be
  testable: it takes its [1mtransport and flush-registration as injected parameters[0m, defaulting to environment detection.
  That lets tests drive buffering, batch thresholds, drop counting, and the circuit breaker against a fake transport and
  a fake clock, with no browser and no network. I'm calling it out here because it's a design decision, not just a
  testing one.

  The test that must exist regardless of how much else we write: [3ma password placed in [38;5;153mctx[39m never appears anywhere in the
[0m  [3mpayload handed to the transport.[0m That's the assertion protecting the thing you'd most regret.

  This repo currently has no test runner, no linter, no formatter, and no [38;5;153mscripts[39m block. Setting tooling up is never
  cheaper than right now, before the code exists — so this is the moment to decide. My recommendations:

  - [1mUnit tests: Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m + [38;5;153mnode:assert[0m, run via [38;5;153mnode --test[39m. Zero dependencies, which matches the
    repo's current posture, and it handles ESM natively.
  - [1mLint + format: Biome.[0m One devDependency and one binary covering both, rather than the ESLint + Prettier pair. It can
    also enforce the static-[38;5;153mmsg[39m rule area we care about via no-template-literal restrictions if we want to go that far
    later.
  - [1mEnd-to-end: skip.[0m It'd mean a browser driver and a fake collector endpoint to test a login form that's currently a
    stub returning [38;5;153m{ success: true }[39m. That's a lot of machinery guarding very little.
  - [1mFuzz/mutation: skip.[0m Nothing here has the kind of input surface that repays it.

